## What the Cumulant Map Records

- \(T_L(P)\), the truncated cumulant vector, collects all mixed cumulants of \(X\) and \(Y\) from orders \(2\) through \(L\).
- Each source contributes one order-specific cumulant weight times a monomial in its loading direction.
- The forward and reverse cumulant maps are polynomial maps from loading slopes and source cumulant weights to \(T_L(P)\).
- A fiber is the set of parameters that produce the same truncated cumulant vector.
- An arrow image variety is the set of cumulant vectors one arrow convention can reach, closed up in the Zariski sense.
- The target is opposite-fiber emptiness: a cumulant vector from one arrow has no full representation under the other arrow.

---

## The Key Idea

- At order \(2m+2\), the cumulant blocks behave like a simultaneous binary-form decomposition with \(m+2\) loading directions.
- Apolarity here means: a form \(q\) is apolar to the blocks when the differential operator \(q(\partial)\) kills every retained cumulant form, which happens exactly when \(q\) vanishes on the loading directions.
- Apolar equations recover the unordered support of those loading directions on a generic locus.
- The recovered support carries an axis signature: forward support contains the vertical-axis pattern, while reverse support contains the horizontal-axis pattern.
- Once the support is recovered, the opposite axis convention would require an incompatible axis signature.
- This is the reason cumulants separate arrows at the representation level.

