## Why the Novelty Wins

- A covariance-level comparison sees only second-order structure and cannot recover the finite loading support.
- A direct same-arrow parameter recovery target is too strong because direct and middle slots can swap while preserving cumulants.
- The paper targets the sharper object: the opposite-arrow fiber under the maintained axis convention.
- The apolar step uses order \(2m+2=2n-2\) with \(n=m+2\) loading directions to recover support.
- That support-level recovery removes the ambiguity that matters for opposite-arrow compatibility, while allowing same-arrow nonuniqueness.

---

## Main Result 3: The Exceptional Set

@informal thm:exceptional-locus-codimension-one: At order \(2m+2\), the closure of cumulant vectors compatible with both arrow conventions has codimension exactly one inside each arrow image variety.

@formal thm:exceptional-locus-codimension-one

