## Why the Apolar Result Is True

- Independence turns the cumulant of \((X,Y)\) into a sum of source-level contributions.
- Each cumulant order becomes a binary form built from the same loading directions with different source weights.
- Across the retained orders, the common annihilator identifies the finite support of loading directions.
- On the generic locus, the annihilator is unique up to scale and squarefree.
- The recovered support reveals which coordinate axis is fixed by the representation.

---

## Why Opposite Compatibility Is Exceptional

- A cumulant vector compatible with both arrows must admit two simultaneous decompositions with different fixed-axis conventions.
- The comparison is made after quotienting only the admissible relabelling of middle source slots.
- The axis slots remain distinguished, so direct-axis and fixed-axis constraints still impose algebraic restrictions.
- The resulting compatibility locus is a hypersurface in each arrow image variety.
- For \(m=1\) and \(m=2\), the paper records explicit incidence systems for this compatibility event.

---

## Takeaways

- Higher-order cumulants establish generic representation-level arrow separation in a fixed, axis-normalized latent-source class.
- Order \(2m+2\) recovers the unordered loading-slope support and excludes the opposite-arrow fiber on generic loci.
- Same-arrow parametrizations retain structured ambiguity beyond middle-source relabelling.
- The opposite-arrow compatibility locus has codimension exactly one at the apolar order.
- For \(m\ge3\), real feasible generic separation holds already at order \(2m+1\).
- The results characterize population information available before finite-sample estimation.
